# How to add a simple component

Pattern: theme-aware, single root element. Use this for `AppButton`, `AppInput`, `AppText`, `AppCard`, `AppBadge`, `AppIcon`, etc.

Every design-system component is prefixed `App` (see AGENTS.md Part 2 — Conventions). Examples below use `AppButton` as the canonical case; substitute `App<Name>` for your component.

## Step-by-step

1. Create `src/components/App<Name>/`:

   ```
   src/components/App<Name>/
   ├── index.tsx
   ├── App<Name>.module.scss
   ├── App<Name>.types.ts
   ├── App<Name>.stories.tsx
   ├── README.md
   └── assets/                 ← only if the component has colocated SVGs
   ```

2. Define props in `App<Name>.types.ts`. Always accept `className` on the root for downstream styling:

   ```ts
   import type { ButtonHTMLAttributes, ReactNode } from 'react'

   export type AppButtonVariant = 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'ghost'
   export type AppButtonSize = 'small' | 'medium' | 'large'

   export type AppButtonProps = {
     variant?: AppButtonVariant
     size?: AppButtonSize
     isLoading?: boolean
     children: ReactNode
     className?: string
   } & ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>
   ```

3. Write styles in `App<Name>.module.scss`. **Never** hardcode hex, raw spacing, raw radii, or raw breakpoints — always go through tokens. Reference `src/styles/settings.scss` and the breakpoint mixins in `src/styles/mixins.scss`:

   ```scss
   @import 'mixins';

   .root {
     display: inline-flex;
     align-items: center;
     justify-content: center;
     padding: var(--size-sm) var(--size-md);
     font-family: var(--font-family-base);
     font-size: var(--font-size-md);
     font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
     line-height: var(--line-height-tight);
     color: var(--color-text-on-primary);
     background-color: var(--color-primary);
     border: none;
     border-radius: var(--radius-md);
     cursor: pointer;
     transition: background-color 0.15s ease;

     @media (hover: hover) {
       &:hover {
         background-color: var(--color-primary-hover);
       }
     }

     &:focus-visible {
       outline: 2px solid var(--color-focus-ring);
       outline-offset: 2px;
     }

     &:active {
       background-color: var(--color-primary-pressed);
     }

     &[disabled],
     &[aria-disabled='true'] {
       cursor: not-allowed;
       opacity: 0.5;
     }

     @include b-up(md) {
       padding: var(--size-md) var(--size-lg);
     }
   }

   .secondary {
     color: var(--color-text);
     background-color: var(--color-bg-elevated);
   }

   .ghost {
     color: var(--color-text);
     background-color: transparent;
   }

   .small {
     padding: var(--size-xs) var(--size-sm);
     font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
   }

   .large {
     padding: var(--size-md) var(--size-xl);
     font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
   }
   ```

   Single-root design-system components keep `.root` plus flat variant classes as above. **Multi-element modules** (feature/layout components like `Footer`, or any module with child elements) use `&_` nesting instead: name the block after the component and nest every child class as `&_element` — `.footer { &_card { … } }` compiles to `.footer_card`, consumed as `s.footer_card`.

4. Implement `index.tsx` as an **arrow-function component** (enforced by ESLint). Use `classnames` aliased as `cn` (rule 14):

   ```tsx
   import cn from 'classnames'

   import s from './AppButton.module.scss'

   import type { AppButtonProps } from './AppButton.types'

   export const AppButton = ({
     variant = 'primary',
     size = 'medium',
     isLoading = false,
     disabled,
     className,
     children,
     ...rest
   }: AppButtonProps) => (
     <button
       type="button"
       className={cn(s.root, s[variant], s[size], className)}
       disabled={disabled || isLoading}
       aria-busy={isLoading || undefined}
       {...rest}
     >
       {children}
     </button>
   )
   ```

5. Stories in `App<Name>.stories.tsx` (CSF3, types from `@storybook/nextjs-vite`). The Storybook title path mirrors the component name — `Components/App<Name>`.

   ```tsx
   import type { Meta, StoryObj } from '@storybook/nextjs-vite'

   import { AppButton } from '.'

   const meta = {
     title: 'Components/AppButton',
     component: AppButton,
     args: { children: 'Primary action' },
   } satisfies Meta<typeof AppButton>

   export default meta
   type Story = StoryObj<typeof meta>

   export const Primary: Story = {}
   export const Secondary: Story = { args: { variant: 'secondary' } }
   export const Disabled: Story = { args: { disabled: true } }
   export const Loading: Story = { args: { isLoading: true } }
   ```

6. Export from `src/components/index.ts` so consumers can do `import { AppButton } from '~components'`:

   ```ts
   export { AppButton } from './AppButton'
   export type {
     AppButtonProps,
     AppButtonVariant,
     AppButtonSize,
   } from './AppButton/AppButton.types'
   ```

7. Add the component to the in-app showcase:
   - Append an entry to the catalog used by `src/app/showcase/page.tsx` (list + search). Use `status: 'available'` plus a stable `slug` — slugs stay unprefixed (`button`, `icon`, `modal`) for URL readability; the `name` field holds the full `App<Name>` identifier.
   - Create `src/app/showcase/<slug>/page.tsx` rendering each variant + state for visual inspection. Keep it minimal: section per variant, section per state.

8. Write `README.md` per the template in `05-quality-checks.mdc`. Heading is `App<Name>`.

9. Run `npm run lint` and verify the new files have zero warnings before handing off.
